"[2511.08823] DT-NVS: Diffusion Transformers for Novel View Synthesis (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08823)\nciteturn5973view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08823\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:DT-NVS: Diffusion Transformers for Novel View Synthesis\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Wonbong Jang , cite6†Jonathan Tremblay , cite7†Lourdes Agapito L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled DT-NVS: Diffusion Transformers for Novel View Synthesis, by Wonbong Jang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Generating novel views of a natural scene, e.g., every-day scenes both indoors and outdoors, from a single view is an under-explored problem, even though it is an organic extension to the object-centric novel view synthesis. Existing diffusion-based approaches focus rather on small camera movements in real scenes or only consider unnatural object-centric scenes, limiting their potential applications in real-world settings.\nL17: In this paper we move away from these constrained regimes and propose a 3D diffusion model trained with image-only losses on a large-scale dataset of real-world, multi-category, unaligned, and casually acquired videos of everyday scenes. We propose DT-NVS, a 3D-aware diffusion model for generalized novel view synthesis that exploits a transformer-based architecture backbone.\nL18: We make significant contributions to transformer and self-attention architectures to translate images to 3d representations, and novel camera conditioning strategies to allow training on real-world unaligned datasets. In addition, we introduce a novel training paradigm swapping the role of reference frame between the conditioning image and the sampled noisy input.\nL19: We evaluate our approach on the 3D task of generalized novel view synthesis from a single input image and show improvements over state-of-the-art 3D aware diffusion models and deterministic approaches, while generating diverse outputs.\nL20: Comments:  | 14 pages\nL21: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.08823 [cs.CV]\nL23:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.08823v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL24:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08823†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Wonbong Jang Mr [cite13†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 22:40:00 UTC (3,510 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled DT-NVS: Diffusion Transformers for Novel View Synthesis, by Wonbong Jang and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L46: \nL47: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite21†cs L52: cite22†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bi